1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,240
What if one of the world's most

2
00:00:02,240 --> 00:00:05,749
important industries depended on an island

3
00:00:05,749 --> 00:00:07,968
smaller than Switzerland?

4
00:00:08,217 --> 00:00:11,620
Taiwan covers about 36,000 square

5
00:00:11,620 --> 00:00:13,127
kilometers.

6
00:00:13,137 --> 00:00:16,035
Yet most of the world's most advanced

7
00:00:16,035 --> 00:00:18,245
chips are made right here.

8
00:00:28,883 --> 00:00:31,611
So how did a small island become

9
00:00:31,611 --> 00:00:34,333
the center of chipmaking?

10
00:00:34,483 --> 00:00:37,965
It matters because almost everything we

11
00:00:37,965 --> 00:00:39,677
use now runs on chips,

12
00:00:39,677 --> 00:00:42,308
and someone has to make them.

13
00:00:42,550 --> 00:00:43,749
For a long time,

14
00:00:43,749 --> 00:00:46,550
the chip business had one rule.

15
00:00:46,650 --> 00:00:48,178
If you designed chips,

16
00:00:48,178 --> 00:00:50,393
you also built the factory that made

17
00:00:50,393 --> 00:00:50,814
them,

18
00:00:50,814 --> 00:00:52,240
called a fab.

19
00:00:52,240 --> 00:00:54,910
And fabs were brutally expensive.

20
00:00:54,910 --> 00:00:57,361
So only giants could play.

21
00:00:57,617 --> 00:00:59,464
In 1987,

22
00:00:59,464 --> 00:01:01,690
with backing from Taiwan's government and

23
00:01:01,690 --> 00:01:03,448
the Dutch company Philips,

24
00:01:03,448 --> 00:01:06,119
a new company called TSMC tried something

25
00:01:06,119 --> 00:01:07,387
different.

26
00:01:07,517 --> 00:01:09,267
It would design nothing.

27
00:01:09,267 --> 00:01:11,485
It would only manufacture chips,

28
00:01:11,485 --> 00:01:12,757
for anyone.

29
00:01:12,997 --> 00:01:15,468
It became the world's first dedicated chip

30
00:01:15,468 --> 00:01:16,517
foundry.

31
00:01:16,697 --> 00:01:19,547
That one idea rewired the industry.

32
00:01:19,547 --> 00:01:21,254
A small team with a great design

33
00:01:21,254 --> 00:01:23,987
no longer needed its own factory.

34
00:01:24,187 --> 00:01:24,853
Nvidia,

35
00:01:24,853 --> 00:01:27,217
founded in 1993,

36
00:01:27,217 --> 00:01:29,260
became one of those designers,

37
00:01:29,260 --> 00:01:31,467
and so did Apple and AMD.

38
00:01:31,627 --> 00:01:33,710
They focus on design and send their

39
00:01:33,710 --> 00:01:35,467
blueprints to Taiwan.

40
00:01:35,557 --> 00:01:37,723
The timeline turned into a network,

41
00:01:37,723 --> 00:01:39,361
and Taiwan sat at the center of

42
00:01:39,361 --> 00:01:40,143
it.

43
00:01:40,383 --> 00:01:44,027
Today TSMC takes about 72 percent of

44
00:01:44,027 --> 00:01:46,511
all the revenue earned by the world's

45
00:01:46,511 --> 00:01:49,043
top ten contract chipmakers.

46
00:01:49,173 --> 00:01:51,181
In twenty twenty-five alone,

47
00:01:51,181 --> 00:01:54,433
it brought in 122 billion dollars.

48
00:01:54,433 --> 00:01:56,120
And at the end of that year,

49
00:01:56,120 --> 00:01:58,726
77 percent of its wafer revenue came

50
00:01:58,726 --> 00:02:00,841
from its most advanced processes,

51
00:02:00,841 --> 00:02:03,363
seven nanometers and smaller.

52
00:02:03,553 --> 00:02:06,133
Taiwan didn't just join the industry.

53
00:02:06,133 --> 00:02:07,865
It took over the hardest part of

54
00:02:07,865 --> 00:02:08,658
it.

55
00:02:08,917 --> 00:02:11,834
But if the idea was that simple,

56
00:02:11,834 --> 00:02:14,791
why didn't everyone copy it?

57
00:02:20,450 --> 00:02:23,443
Why can't every country just build its

58
00:02:23,443 --> 00:02:25,540
own chip factories?

59
00:02:25,740 --> 00:02:26,909
If they could,

60
00:02:26,909 --> 00:02:30,208
Taiwan would not be special at all.

61
00:02:30,450 --> 00:02:32,540
Let me draw what it takes.

62
00:02:32,540 --> 00:02:33,016
First,

63
00:02:33,016 --> 00:02:34,996
engineers design the chip,

64
00:02:34,996 --> 00:02:37,350
billions of tiny switches.

65
00:02:37,390 --> 00:02:39,655
Then a fab prints that design onto

66
00:02:39,655 --> 00:02:41,392
a silicon wafer,

67
00:02:41,392 --> 00:02:42,625
layer after layer,

68
00:02:42,625 --> 00:02:43,980
using light.

69
00:02:44,140 --> 00:02:46,315
Then the wafer is cut into chips,

70
00:02:46,315 --> 00:02:48,051
packaged with memory,

71
00:02:48,051 --> 00:02:49,310
and tested.

72
00:02:49,370 --> 00:02:51,175
Every step has to be close to

73
00:02:51,175 --> 00:02:52,177
perfect.

74
00:02:52,417 --> 00:02:54,937
Here is the first obstacle.

75
00:02:55,097 --> 00:02:57,127
To print the smallest features,

76
00:02:57,127 --> 00:02:59,180
a fab needs extreme ultraviolet

77
00:02:59,180 --> 00:03:01,377
lithography machines.

78
00:03:01,547 --> 00:03:04,300
Only one company in the world makes

79
00:03:04,300 --> 00:03:04,775
them,

80
00:03:04,775 --> 00:03:07,127
the Dutch firm A S M L.

81
00:03:07,457 --> 00:03:08,903
In twenty twenty-five,

82
00:03:08,903 --> 00:03:11,220
it shipped 48 of them.

83
00:03:11,483 --> 00:03:14,803
And owning the machines is not enough.

84
00:03:14,893 --> 00:03:16,772
The real secret is yield,

85
00:03:16,772 --> 00:03:18,951
the share of chips on each wafer

86
00:03:18,951 --> 00:03:20,913
that actually work.

87
00:03:21,073 --> 00:03:23,479
Raising yield takes years of practice,

88
00:03:23,479 --> 00:03:25,156
thousands of engineers,

89
00:03:25,156 --> 00:03:27,943
and endless learning from mistakes.

90
00:03:28,123 --> 00:03:29,697
A brand-new factory,

91
00:03:29,697 --> 00:03:32,183
even with the very same machines,

92
00:03:32,183 --> 00:03:33,780
can take a long time to match

93
00:03:33,780 --> 00:03:36,433
the yields of an experienced one.

94
00:03:36,543 --> 00:03:38,409
That know-how is hard to buy,

95
00:03:38,409 --> 00:03:40,898
and even harder to copy.

96
00:03:41,150 --> 00:03:44,150
Then there is the money.

97
00:03:44,430 --> 00:03:46,956
TSMC planned to spend more than 50

98
00:03:46,956 --> 00:03:49,929
billion dollars on factories and equipment

99
00:03:49,929 --> 00:03:52,510
in twenty twenty-six alone.

100
00:03:52,750 --> 00:03:54,357
Across the industry,

101
00:03:54,357 --> 00:03:57,152
chipmakers are expected to invest about

102
00:03:57,152 --> 00:04:00,343
2.3 trillion dollars in new fabs between

103
00:04:00,343 --> 00:04:03,505
twenty twenty-four and twenty thirty-two.

104
00:04:03,750 --> 00:04:06,526
Taiwan does not make most of the

105
00:04:06,526 --> 00:04:07,820
world's chips.

106
00:04:07,890 --> 00:04:10,925
In twenty twenty-two it had 18 percent

107
00:04:10,925 --> 00:04:13,200
of all chipmaking capacity,

108
00:04:13,200 --> 00:04:14,720
less than China.

109
00:04:15,190 --> 00:04:17,814
But for the most advanced logic chips,

110
00:04:17,814 --> 00:04:19,716
below ten nanometers,

111
00:04:19,716 --> 00:04:22,290
Taiwan had 69 percent.

112
00:04:22,600 --> 00:04:25,170
South Korea had 31 percent.

113
00:04:25,380 --> 00:04:27,901
The United States had none.

114
00:04:28,150 --> 00:04:31,280
And chips are not one market.

115
00:04:31,320 --> 00:04:33,788
Memory is a different game,

116
00:04:33,788 --> 00:04:35,259
led by South Korea,

117
00:04:35,259 --> 00:04:37,253
which had about half of the world's

118
00:04:37,253 --> 00:04:39,100
DRAM capacity.

119
00:04:39,270 --> 00:04:40,779
Advanced packaging,

120
00:04:40,779 --> 00:04:42,943
the step that joins chips and memory,

121
00:04:42,943 --> 00:04:45,730
has become a bottleneck of its own.

122
00:04:45,810 --> 00:04:47,952
So Taiwan's edge is narrow,

123
00:04:47,952 --> 00:04:50,721
but it sits at the very top.

124
00:04:50,983 --> 00:04:53,773
Which raises the next question.

125
00:04:53,883 --> 00:04:56,193
Who depends on that top?

126
00:05:01,850 --> 00:05:05,410
So who actually depends on Taiwan?

127
00:05:05,410 --> 00:05:07,729
It matters because dependence is where

128
00:05:07,729 --> 00:05:09,329
economic power,

129
00:05:09,329 --> 00:05:10,929
and economic risk,

130
00:05:10,929 --> 00:05:11,993
come from.

131
00:05:12,250 --> 00:05:13,620
Let me draw it.

132
00:05:13,620 --> 00:05:15,140
Here is Taiwan.

133
00:05:15,140 --> 00:05:15,882
And here,

134
00:05:15,882 --> 00:05:17,646
across the Pacific,

135
00:05:17,646 --> 00:05:19,154
is California,

136
00:05:19,154 --> 00:05:20,407
home to Nvidia,

137
00:05:20,407 --> 00:05:22,030
Apple and AMD.

138
00:05:22,090 --> 00:05:23,190
They design chips,

139
00:05:23,190 --> 00:05:25,212
but they don't make their most advanced

140
00:05:25,212 --> 00:05:26,710
chips themselves.

141
00:05:26,840 --> 00:05:28,540
TSMC does.

142
00:05:28,540 --> 00:05:31,158
So the lines run from California to

143
00:05:31,158 --> 00:05:31,848
Taiwan,

144
00:05:31,848 --> 00:05:34,590
and back again as finished chips.

145
00:05:34,850 --> 00:05:37,430
How concentrated is it?

146
00:05:37,900 --> 00:05:39,807
In twenty twenty-five,

147
00:05:39,807 --> 00:05:43,456
TSMC's largest customer alone made up 19

148
00:05:43,456 --> 00:05:45,364
percent of its revenue,

149
00:05:45,364 --> 00:05:49,060
and the second largest 17 percent.

150
00:05:49,490 --> 00:05:52,163
Nvidia's chief executive has said Nvidia

151
00:05:52,163 --> 00:05:53,354
is the biggest,

152
00:05:53,354 --> 00:05:56,271
and the second is widely reported to

153
00:05:56,271 --> 00:05:57,600
be Apple.

154
00:05:58,110 --> 00:06:01,092
But notice that this dependence runs both

155
00:06:01,092 --> 00:06:01,990
ways.

156
00:06:02,220 --> 00:06:05,784
TSMC needs its biggest customers almost as

157
00:06:05,784 --> 00:06:08,004
much as they need TSMC.

158
00:06:08,250 --> 00:06:10,870
Now follow the chips further.

159
00:06:11,120 --> 00:06:14,165
Cloud companies in the United States,

160
00:06:14,165 --> 00:06:16,979
Europe and Asia fill their data centers

161
00:06:16,979 --> 00:06:19,370
with AI chips made in Taiwan.

162
00:06:19,600 --> 00:06:20,753
Phone makers,

163
00:06:20,753 --> 00:06:24,310
PC makers and carmakers buy chips too.

164
00:06:24,500 --> 00:06:26,187
In twenty twenty-five,

165
00:06:26,187 --> 00:06:29,806
Taiwan's exports hit a record 640 billion

166
00:06:29,806 --> 00:06:30,515
dollars,

167
00:06:30,515 --> 00:06:33,853
up 35 percent in a single year.

168
00:06:34,117 --> 00:06:36,827
Here is the key distinction.

169
00:06:36,827 --> 00:06:39,331
Companies that design chips depend on the

170
00:06:39,331 --> 00:06:41,297
companies that make them.

171
00:06:41,347 --> 00:06:44,293
And the makers depend on Dutch machines,

172
00:06:44,293 --> 00:06:47,512
Japanese materials and American design

173
00:06:47,512 --> 00:06:48,607
software.

174
00:06:48,747 --> 00:06:51,416
Everyone depends on someone.

175
00:06:51,650 --> 00:06:54,624
But not every industry depends on Taiwan

176
00:06:54,624 --> 00:06:55,800
equally.

177
00:06:55,800 --> 00:06:58,029
A cutting-edge AI chip or a new

178
00:06:58,029 --> 00:07:00,507
phone processor needs the most advanced

179
00:07:00,507 --> 00:07:01,473
factories,

180
00:07:01,473 --> 00:07:04,510
and that is where Taiwan dominates.

181
00:07:04,510 --> 00:07:06,718
A car or a washing machine mostly

182
00:07:06,718 --> 00:07:07,801
uses older,

183
00:07:07,801 --> 00:07:09,788
simpler chips that are made in many

184
00:07:09,788 --> 00:07:10,790
countries.

185
00:07:10,790 --> 00:07:11,841
Even so,

186
00:07:11,841 --> 00:07:13,996
a missing cheap chip can still stop

187
00:07:13,996 --> 00:07:15,943
a car factory.

188
00:07:16,183 --> 00:07:18,165
So what would happen if the flow

189
00:07:18,165 --> 00:07:20,395
of chips from Taiwan were suddenly

190
00:07:20,395 --> 00:07:21,987
interrupted?

191
00:07:27,650 --> 00:07:29,810
Here is a thought experiment.

192
00:07:29,900 --> 00:07:32,234
What if chip shipments from Taiwan were

193
00:07:32,234 --> 00:07:34,390
seriously disrupted?

194
00:07:34,560 --> 00:07:35,439
To be clear,

195
00:07:35,439 --> 00:07:38,123
this is a hypothetical scenario,

196
00:07:38,123 --> 00:07:39,850
not a prediction.

197
00:07:40,117 --> 00:07:42,517
We do have one real clue.

198
00:07:42,517 --> 00:07:43,906
In twenty twenty-one,

199
00:07:43,906 --> 00:07:46,707
a chip shortage caused by the pandemic,

200
00:07:46,707 --> 00:07:47,860
not by Taiwan,

201
00:07:47,860 --> 00:07:49,637
hit carmakers hard.

202
00:07:49,767 --> 00:07:51,764
One forecast put the cost to the

203
00:07:51,764 --> 00:07:55,047
global auto industry at 210 billion

204
00:07:55,047 --> 00:07:56,578
dollars in lost revenue,

205
00:07:56,578 --> 00:07:59,839
and 7.7 million vehicles that were never

206
00:07:59,839 --> 00:08:00,661
built.

207
00:08:00,917 --> 00:08:04,007
Now imagine a disruption at the source.

208
00:08:04,007 --> 00:08:06,367
Advanced chip supply stops.

209
00:08:06,367 --> 00:08:08,607
New phones and computers slip.

210
00:08:08,607 --> 00:08:11,507
Cloud companies can't add AI servers.

211
00:08:11,507 --> 00:08:13,697
Carmakers idle their lines.

212
00:08:13,697 --> 00:08:14,582
Prices rise,

213
00:08:14,582 --> 00:08:16,625
and the shock spreads to industries that

214
00:08:16,625 --> 00:08:19,267
never thought of themselves as tech.

215
00:08:19,347 --> 00:08:21,472
A shortage of the most advanced chips

216
00:08:21,472 --> 00:08:23,788
would be very hard to replace quickly,

217
00:08:23,788 --> 00:08:26,338
because the alternative factories simply

218
00:08:26,338 --> 00:08:28,638
don't exist yet at that scale.

219
00:08:28,883 --> 00:08:30,993
Let me draw that chain.

220
00:08:30,993 --> 00:08:32,593
Taiwan's fabs.

221
00:08:32,593 --> 00:08:33,883
Then packaging.

222
00:08:33,883 --> 00:08:35,543
Then the device makers.

223
00:08:35,543 --> 00:08:38,283
Then the stores and the data centers.

224
00:08:38,413 --> 00:08:40,629
Now break the very first link,

225
00:08:40,629 --> 00:08:42,873
and watch the shock travel down the

226
00:08:42,873 --> 00:08:43,563
line.

227
00:08:43,563 --> 00:08:47,154
Every box after the break turns red.

228
00:08:47,417 --> 00:08:49,317
How big could it get?

229
00:08:49,317 --> 00:08:50,812
In twenty twenty-four,

230
00:08:50,812 --> 00:08:53,627
Bloomberg Economics estimated that a war

231
00:08:53,627 --> 00:08:55,793
over Taiwan could cut world output by

232
00:08:55,793 --> 00:08:58,414
about 10 percent in the first year,

233
00:08:58,414 --> 00:09:00,997
about 10 trillion dollars.

234
00:09:00,997 --> 00:09:03,277
Even a blockade could cost about 5

235
00:09:03,277 --> 00:09:04,307
percent.

236
00:09:04,447 --> 00:09:06,684
Bloomberg stresses these are scenarios,

237
00:09:06,684 --> 00:09:09,769
and they depend heavily on assumptions.

238
00:09:10,017 --> 00:09:13,075
Numbers like that explain why governments

239
00:09:13,075 --> 00:09:15,521
are now racing to make chips at

240
00:09:15,521 --> 00:09:16,426
home.

241
00:09:22,083 --> 00:09:24,583
Can the world spread the risk?

242
00:09:24,743 --> 00:09:26,890
It matters because diversification is

243
00:09:26,890 --> 00:09:27,587
expensive,

244
00:09:27,587 --> 00:09:28,903
and it is slow.

245
00:09:31,083 --> 00:09:32,963
Let's map the race.

246
00:09:32,983 --> 00:09:35,892
America's CHIPS Act offers about 53

247
00:09:35,892 --> 00:09:38,294
billion dollars for semiconductors,

248
00:09:38,294 --> 00:09:40,541
39 billion dollars of it for

249
00:09:40,541 --> 00:09:42,243
manufacturing.

250
00:09:42,513 --> 00:09:46,345
TSMC plans to invest 165 billion dollars

251
00:09:46,345 --> 00:09:48,333
in Arizona.

252
00:09:48,393 --> 00:09:49,361
In Japan,

253
00:09:49,361 --> 00:09:52,638
its Kumamoto venture costs more than 20

254
00:09:52,638 --> 00:09:54,232
billion dollars.

255
00:09:54,483 --> 00:09:55,552
In Germany,

256
00:09:55,552 --> 00:09:57,515
a TSMC joint venture is rising in

257
00:09:57,515 --> 00:09:58,148
Dresden,

258
00:09:58,148 --> 00:10:00,810
and Europe wants 20 percent of global

259
00:10:00,810 --> 00:10:03,373
chip production by twenty thirty.

260
00:10:03,463 --> 00:10:05,541
South Korea keeps pouring money into

261
00:10:05,541 --> 00:10:07,723
memory and its own foundries.

262
00:10:07,723 --> 00:10:10,567
And Taiwan keeps building its newest fabs

263
00:10:10,567 --> 00:10:11,736
at home.

264
00:10:11,983 --> 00:10:13,833
But here is the limit.

265
00:10:13,903 --> 00:10:15,754
Even with all this spending,

266
00:10:15,754 --> 00:10:18,331
the forecast from the industry's own trade

267
00:10:18,331 --> 00:10:20,440
group and Boston Consulting Group still

268
00:10:20,440 --> 00:10:23,767
has Taiwan holding 47 percent of advanced

269
00:10:23,767 --> 00:10:26,790
logic capacity in twenty thirty-two,

270
00:10:26,790 --> 00:10:30,483
with the United States at 28 percent.

271
00:10:30,603 --> 00:10:33,373
Diversification reduces the risk.

272
00:10:33,373 --> 00:10:35,009
It doesn't remove it.

273
00:10:35,250 --> 00:10:36,850
Why so slow?

274
00:10:36,850 --> 00:10:39,510
Because the hard part isn't the building.

275
00:10:39,540 --> 00:10:41,116
It is the engineers,

276
00:10:41,116 --> 00:10:42,007
the suppliers,

277
00:10:42,007 --> 00:10:43,835
and the years of yield learning that

278
00:10:43,835 --> 00:10:45,120
come with them.

279
00:10:45,400 --> 00:10:48,040
Taiwan has spent decades on that.

280
00:10:48,080 --> 00:10:50,140
A new fab abroad can be built

281
00:10:50,140 --> 00:10:51,770
in a few years.

282
00:10:51,900 --> 00:10:54,647
Building the whole ecosystem around it

283
00:10:54,647 --> 00:10:56,391
takes much longer.

284
00:10:56,650 --> 00:10:59,460
Taiwan matters not because it makes every

285
00:10:59,460 --> 00:10:59,870
chip,

286
00:10:59,870 --> 00:11:02,442
but because it makes the hardest ones,

287
00:11:02,442 --> 00:11:05,420
at a scale nobody else has matched.

288
00:11:05,870 --> 00:11:08,000
An island smaller than Switzerland ended

289
00:11:08,000 --> 00:11:09,937
up holding one of the most important

290
00:11:09,937 --> 00:11:12,280
